---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Wipe from '../components/Wipe.astro';

const TOC = [
  ['problem', 'The problem with estimating'],
  ['measures', 'What Obelisk measures'],
  ['density', 'Density is the control'],
  ['aged', 'Aged film'],
  ['light', 'Scanning light'],
  ['calibration', 'Calibration in practice'],
];
---
<Base title="How it works" description="Obelisk converts colour negatives through a measured calibration of the film stock on your rig: a density fingerprint, a gated colour matrix, a per-roll film base and exposure as arithmetic.">
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="How it works"
    title="Measure the film. Then convert through the measurement."
    lede="A conversion can only be as consistent as the thing it anchors to. Scene statistics are not stable; the film’s physical behaviour is. This is how Obelisk uses that."
  />

  <div class="wrap doc-grid">
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="toc-h">On this page</p>
      {TOC.map(([id, l]) => <a href={`#${id}`} data-toc={id}>{l}</a>)}
    </nav>

    <article class="doc">
      <section id="problem">
        <h2>The problem with estimating</h2>
        <p>
          Most negative conversion works from the image: it reads each frame’s statistics —
          white point, black point, average colour — and estimates a correction. That approach
          fails in exactly the situations that fill real rolls.
        </p>
        <ul class="points">
          <li><strong>Scenes without neutral content drift.</strong> A red room, a forest, a sunset — average-to-grey logic reads the scene itself as a colour cast and corrects it away.</li>
          <li><strong>Mis-exposed frames are corrected toward the wrong target.</strong> Over-exposed frames come out bright and desaturated; night shots get lifted to grey.</li>
          <li><strong>Frame-by-frame analysis drifts frame by frame.</strong> Two photos taken seconds apart render differently because something in the scene changed the histogram.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Obelisk has no per-image auto-balance. Every frame converts through a fixed, measured
          calibration, so a result can be reproduced exactly — frame to frame, roll to roll,
          operator to operator.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="measures">
        <h2>What Obelisk measures</h2>
        <p>
          A short calibration session — a colour chart photographed on one roll of the stock,
          bracketed in one-stop steps — captures how that film responds on your light and your
          camera.
        </p>
        <div class="mcards">
          <div class="mcard"><span class="data">Fingerprint</span><h3>Neutral at every density</h3><p>Dozens of measured points along each channel’s response, across ±3 stops. Grey reads grey in the shadows, midtones and highlights — removing the casts that a single white balance move can never fix.</p></div>
          <div class="mcard"><span class="data">Matrix</span><h3>Gated colour correction</h3><p>The chart’s colour patches fit a cross-channel correction for the overlap between the film’s dyes and the camera’s filters. It must preserve neutrals and pass statistical quality gates, or it is not used.</p></div>
          <div class="mcard"><span class="data">Base</span><h3>The film’s own zero</h3><p>Each roll’s unexposed base is measured from the clear film between frames, agreed across the roll, and subtracted in density space. The orange mask and the scan light cancel exactly.</p></div>
          <div class="mcard"><span class="data">Exposure</span><h3>Arithmetic, not guesswork</h3><p>On a negative, an exposure error is a uniform density offset. It is removed with a gain that preserves colour ratios, placing every frame at the same print density.</p></div>
        </div>
        <figure class="figure">
          <div class="pair">
            <img src="/assets/fp_fingerprint.svg" alt="Measured per-channel density fingerprint" loading="lazy" />
            <img src="/assets/fp_matrix.svg" alt="Fitted colour correction acting on each hue" loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            From a production calibration (Kodak Gold 200) — the instrument’s own readouts.
            Left: the neutral axis of the measured fingerprint — how far each channel deviates
            from neutral at every density, fitted through the calibration points; dashed curves
            are the same stock’s measured <em>aged</em> response. Right: the fitted colour
            correction as its action on each hue — the dashed ring is the uncorrected input, each
            arrow is where the correction moves that hue.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="density">
        <h2>Density is the control</h2>
        <p>
          Brightness works the way a printer or minilab handles it: as print density. One control
          moves the frame through a fixed print curve with the blacks anchored — no black-point
          stretching, no re-fitted histogram. A density change on screen behaves like an exposure
          change at the enlarger, so contrast and colour hold while the frame gets lighter or
          darker.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same maths runs in two places: on the GPU for live grading as you drag, and in the
          export path at full resolution. They are kept in parity, so what you approve is what
          you deliver.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="aged">
        <h2>Aged film, measured</h2>
        <p>
          Aged film — expired stock, or exposed rolls that waited years for development — does
          not simply fade. Its colour balance shifts unevenly across the tonal range: shadows
          drift toward magenta while highlights drift toward teal. Because the error bends with
          density, no white-balance move can remove it. Auto-balancing converters average the
          two casts against each other and leave both in the frame.
        </p>
        <p>
          Obelisk calibrates the same stock twice — once fresh, and once from film deliberately
          aged before development — capturing how ageing bends each channel’s response at every
          density. The correction is a single per-roll control: zero is the fresh calibration,
          and raising it applies the measured ageing shift in reverse until the roll’s neutrals
          line up. Shadows and highlights return to neutral together, because the correction
          follows the same curve the ageing did.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="light">
        <h2>Scanning light, measured at the sensor</h2>
        <p>
          Cameras apply large white-balance gains to raw data before you see anything — on a
          typical body, around ×2.6 on red and ×1.6 on blue. A light that looks balanced on the
          camera’s histogram can leave the sensor badly starved of red, and colour negative film
          blocks most red to begin with. Tuning the light against raw, gain-free sensor values
          gave a substantial improvement in red-channel quality for the same scan.
        </p>
        <div class="stats">
          <div><span class="data">34% → 89%</span><p>Raw red level at clear film base, before and after tuning</p></div>
          <div><span class="data">~2×</span><p>Cleaner red content for the same exposure and film</p></div>
          <div><span class="data">4.58 → 4.74</span><p>Measured white density — red now resolves to the top of its range</p></div>
        </div>
        <figure class="figure">
          <Wipe before="/assets/comp/light_daylight.jpg" after="/assets/comp/light_sensor.jpg"
                beforeLabel="Daylight-balanced" afterLabel="Sensor-balanced" alt="The same negative, converted identically" />
          <figcaption>Drag to compare. The same negative, converted identically; only the scanning light mix differs.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <h3>White light works too</h3>
        <p>
          None of this makes an RGB source a requirement. Obelisk works with an LED panel or a
          light table, because calibration is measured for whichever light you actually use, and
          a white-light profile is as consistent across a roll as an RGB one. Measured on the
          same negatives scanned both ways, narrowband RGB resolves about 20% more separation
          between colours — so RGB extracts more, but white light is a perfectly workable start.
        </p>
        <p class="note">
          One rule holds regardless: a profile belongs to a light source as well as a film stock.
          Scanning the same stock under a different light means one more five-minute chart
          session — and Obelisk flags a roll whose base signature doesn’t match its profile’s light.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="calibration">
        <h2>Calibration in practice</h2>
        <p>Calibration is done once per film stock and light source, and takes about five minutes at the scanning station.</p>
        <ol class="howto">
          <li><strong>Shoot a chart.</strong> Photograph a standard colour chart on one roll of the stock, bracketed in one-stop steps.</li>
          <li><strong>Develop and scan</strong> the roll on your rig, exactly as you scan customer film.</li>
          <li><strong>Point Obelisk at the bracket.</strong> It fits the fingerprint and colour matrix, checks the fit against its quality gates, and records the light, aperture and rig it was made on.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Profiles are portable bundles: back them up, move them to a second scanning station, or
          rebuild one from its stored chart measurements when the fitting improves — without
          rescanning.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="doc-cta card">
        <div>
          <h3>See the results on real frames</h3>
          <p class="muted">Bracket and real-scene comparisons against three other converters, from identical RAW files.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/compare/">Open the comparison <span class="arrow">→</span></a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<script>
  const links = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('[data-toc]')];
  const io = new IntersectionObserver((es) => es.forEach((e) => {
    if (e.isIntersecting) links.forEach((l) => l.classList.toggle('on', l.dataset.toc === e.target.id));
  }), { rootMargin: '-30% 0px -60% 0px' });
  document.querySelectorAll('.doc > section[id]').forEach((s) => io.observe(s));
</script>

<style>
  .doc-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 220px 1fr; gap: clamp(32px, 6vw, 88px); padding-top: 56px; padding-bottom: 96px; }
  .toc { position: sticky; top: 96px; align-self: start; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2px; }
  .toc-h { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--dim); margin-bottom: 12px; }
  .toc a { color: var(--muted); font-size: 14.5px; padding: 6px 0 6px 14px; border-left: 2px solid var(--line); text-decoration: none; transition: color .15s, border-color .15s; }
  .toc a:hover { color: var(--text); }
  .toc a.on { color: var(--text); border-left-color: var(--amber); }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .doc-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding-top: 36px; } .toc { display: none; } }

  .doc { max-width: 760px; min-width: 0; }
  .doc section { padding-bottom: 56px; margin-bottom: 56px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); scroll-margin-top: 96px; }
  .doc h2 { font-size: clamp(26px, 3vw, 34px); margin-bottom: 20px; }
  .doc h3 { font-size: 19px; margin: 32px 0 10px; }
  .doc p { color: var(--text-2); }
  .doc em { color: var(--text); }
  .points { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 24px 0; display: grid; gap: 14px; }
  .points li { padding: 16px 20px; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--r); color: var(--muted); font-size: 15.5px; }
  .points strong { color: var(--text); display: block; margin-bottom: 2px; }
  .mcards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 12px; margin: 28px 0; }
  .mcard { padding: 22px; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--r); }
  .mcard .data { font-size: 11.5px; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .mcard h3 { margin: 10px 0 8px; font-size: 17px; }
  .mcard p { font-size: 15px; color: var(--muted); }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .mcards { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .figure { margin: 30px 0 0; }
  .figure .pair { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 10px; }
  .figure .pair img { border-radius: var(--r); border: 1px solid var(--line); width: 100%; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .figure .pair { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .figure figcaption { font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 12px; }
  .stats { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 12px; margin: 26px 0 0; }
  .stats > div { padding: 18px; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--r); }
  .stats .data { font-size: 20px; }
  .stats p { font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 6px; line-height: 1.45; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .stats { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .note { font-size: 15px; padding: 14px 18px; border-left: 2px solid var(--amber); background: rgba(240,151,63,.05); border-radius: 0 var(--r-sm) var(--r-sm) 0; }
  .howto { padding: 0; margin: 22px 0; list-style: none; counter-reset: s; display: grid; gap: 12px; }
  .howto li { counter-increment: s; position: relative; padding: 2px 0 2px 46px; color: var(--muted); }
  .howto li::before { content: counter(s, decimal-leading-zero); position: absolute; left: 0; top: 2px; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 13px; color: var(--cyan); border: 1px solid var(--line-2); border-radius: 6px; padding: 3px 6px; }
  .howto strong { color: var(--text); }
  .doc-cta { display: flex; gap: 20px; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; padding: 28px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .doc-cta h3 { margin: 0 0 6px; }
</style>
